#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid "Gametype: Toy"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid "Number of possible players: One"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:81
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Ktuberling a simple constructor game suitable for children and adults alike. "
"The idea of the game is based around a once popular doll making concept. A "
"potato was decorated with various small artifacts to make it look more like "
"a tiny person. Ktubeling however, goes much further in terms of content and "
"adds a surprising variety of different themes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:87
#, no-c-format
msgid "How to Use"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:89
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&ktuberling; is a game intended for small children. Of course, it may be "
"suitable for adults who have remained young at heart."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It is a <quote>potato editor</quote>. That means that you can drag and drop "
"eyes, mouths, mustache, and other parts of face and goodies onto a potato-"
"like guy. Similarly, you have other playgrounds with different themes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There is no winner for the game. The only purpose is to make the funniest "
"faces you can."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:108
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There is a museum (like a <quote>Madame Tusseau</quote> gallery) where you "
"can find many funny examples of decorated potatoes, penguins and aquariums. "
"Of course, you can send your own creations to the programmer, <ulink url="
"\"mailto:ebischoff@nerim.net\">&Eric.Bischoff;</ulink>, who will include "
"them in the museum if he gets some spare time."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:116
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&ktuberling; can also <quote>speak</quote>. It will spell out the name of "
"the objects you drag and drop. It will <quote>speak</quote> in a language "
"that you can chose. You can even use it to learn a bit of vocabulary in "
"foreign languages."
msgstr ""

